<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-I-quadratic_surfaces" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Quadratic Surfaces</title>

  <p> A quadric surface is the three-dimensional counterpart to a two-dimensional
    conic section (such as ellipses, parabolas, and hyperbolas), defined as the
    graph of a second-degree polynomial equation in three variables: <m>x</m>,
    <m>y</m>, and <m>z</m>.Their general equation takes the form:
    <md>
        Ax^{2} + By^{2} + Cz^{2} + Dxy + Eyz + Fxz + Gx + Hy + Iz + J = 0
    </md>
    Just as linear equations describe flat planes, quadric surfaces create curved,
    symmetric geometric shapes such as spheres, ellipsoids, paraboloids, and
    hyperboloids. </p>

    <p>
      Quadric surfaces can be classified into several types based on their standard equations, including:
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            Cylinders: <m>x^2 + y^2 = r^2</m>
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Spheres: <m>x^2 + y^2 + z^2 = r^2</m>
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>Ellipsoids: <m>\frac{x^2}{a^2} + \frac{y^2}{b^2} + \frac{z^2}{c^2} = 1</m></li>
        <li>Paraboloids: <m>z = \frac{x^2}{a^2} + \frac{y^2}{b^2}</m> (elliptic) or <m>z = \frac{x^2}{a^2} - \frac{y^2}{b^2}</m> (hyperbolic)</li>
        <li>Hyperboloids: <m>\frac{x^2}{a^2} + \frac{y^2}{b^2} - \frac{z^2}{c^2} = 1</m> (one-sheet) or <m>\frac{x^2}{a^2} + \frac{y^2}{b^2} - \frac{z^2}{c^2} = -1</m> (two-sheet)</li>
      </ul> 
    </p>
